# Why: ONE remaining conflict makes a plain `npm install` fail ERESOLVE on a clean clone —
# @types/node is pinned at 20.6.2, and vite@8 (via @vitejs/plugin-react → vitest) declares
# peerOptional @types/node "^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0". npm treats that as fatal because the package
# is present.
#
# The three React-18 peer pins this file was ORIGINALLY written for are gone (#177): cmdk 1.0.0
# → 1.1.1, next-themes 0.3.0 → 0.4.6, sonner 1.5.0 → 2.0.8 all carry React 19 peer ranges now.
# Do not re-add them to the list above; they resolve clean. Measured: with only @types/node
# bumped to 20.19.0, `npm install --legacy-peer-deps=false` resolves with no ERESOLVE.
#
# MUST live in this directory, not the repo root: npm reads the per-project .npmrc from the PACKAGE
# root only, and a root-level copy reads as `false` — it would look like the fix, pass review, and
# leave the next cloner hitting the same ERESOLVE.
#
# EXIT CONDITION — delete this file once @types/node satisfies vite's range. That bump was left
# out of #177 deliberately: it is a toolchain change with its own type-surface blast radius, not
# part of the React-19 dependency work. It is not free while it stays: legacy-peer-deps=true
# changes resolution for every install forever and makes `npm ci` skip peer validation, so it
# trades one loud bounded failure for a quiet unbounded one — the peer range is the only automated
# signal that a dependency has not been checked against a React major, and nothing detects that
# loss.
legacy-peer-deps=true
